>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> to use a study planner app well, put in your <strong>class timetable, exam dates and homework first</strong>, then let the app turn them into <strong>study blocks spread across the days before each deadline</strong>, and review the plan once a week. It takes about 20 minutes to set up and a few minutes a day to keep up.</p>
  <p>This guide is for any student: high school, college, university or self-taught, on semesters, terms or quarters, anywhere in the world. It explains what a study planner app does, why planning is linked to better grades, a 6-step set-up you can copy, and how our app, <a href={SITE.playUrl} rel="noopener">ClassTrack</a>, handles each step.</p>

  <h2 id="what">What is a study planner app?</h2>
  <p>A <strong>study planner app</strong> is a phone or web app that keeps a student’s <strong>class timetable, homework, exams and study sessions in one place</strong>, and sends reminders so nothing is missed. It differs from a normal to-do list in three ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It understands repeating classes.</strong> You add Monday’s 9:00 lecture once, not every week.</li>
    <li><strong>It links work to subjects.</strong> Homework, grades and exams sit under the class they belong to.</li>
    <li><strong>It plans backwards from deadlines.</strong> An exam is not one task on one day; it is a set of study sessions over many days.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Newer study planners, sometimes called <a href="/ai-study-planner/">AI study planners</a>, add help on top: reading your timetable from a photo, building a topic-by-topic plan, or answering questions like “what should I revise tonight?”.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why students who plan get better results</h2>
  <p>Planning is not just about feeling organised. Research links it to grades and to finishing work on time.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Finding</th><th scope="col">What it means for your planner</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Among 90 college students, short-range planning and time attitudes <strong>predicted cumulative GPA, and did so better than SAT scores</strong> (Britton &amp; Tesser, 1991).</td><td>Planning your week is a skill you can learn, and it matters as much as raw ability.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An estimated <strong>80–95% of college students procrastinate</strong>, and procrastination is linked to lower performance (Steel, 2007).</td><td>Small, dated tasks are easier to start than one big “study for finals”.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Across 94 studies, deciding <strong>when and where</strong> you will act had a medium-to-large effect on reaching goals (Gollwitzer &amp; Sheeran, 2006).</td><td>“Biology, Tuesday 4:30, library” works better than “study biology this week”.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Of ten popular study techniques, only <strong>practice testing and spaced practice</strong> were rated high utility (Dunlosky et al., 2013).</td><td>Use the planner to spread revision over days and to schedule self-tests.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Put simply: a planner helps you do the things that work (start early, space your study, test yourself) without needing to remember to do them. More detail is in <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">what the research says about study techniques</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="who">Who a study planner is for</h2>
  <p>A study planner is useful for any student who has more than one deadline at a time. The set-up is the same everywhere; only the names of your exams change.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Student</th><th scope="col">What they plan</th><th scope="col">Most useful features</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>High school / secondary school</td><td>Daily homework, unit tests, end-of-year or national exams (for example AP, GCSE, A-level, IB or board exams)</td><td>Homework list, exam countdowns, after-school routine</td></tr>
      <tr><td>College and university</td><td>Lectures, labs, assignments, midterms and finals across a semester, term or quarter</td><td>Class timetable, assignment due dates, grades and GPA</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exam preparation</td><td>One big test months away (for example SAT, finals, a professional or entrance exam)</td><td>Topic-by-topic study plan, revision rounds, daily blocks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Self-learners and working students</td><td>Online courses, certifications, a new skill around a job</td><td>AI learning plan, habits, reminders</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="setup">How to set up a study planner app in 6 steps</h2>
  <p>Do these steps in order. Each one gives the next step the information it needs.</p>
  {steps.map((s, i) => (
    <>
      <h3 id={s.id}>Step {i + 1}: {s.name}</h3>
      <p>{s.text}</p>
    </>
  ))}
  <p><strong>Tips for each step:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Timetable:</strong> add rooms and online class links now; you will thank yourself on a busy morning. No app yet? Use the free <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a> and export it to your calendar.</li>
    <li><strong>Exams:</strong> add even the small tests. The <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> shows study days left, not just calendar days.</li>
    <li><strong>Homework:</strong> set the due date one day earlier than the real one. That day is your buffer. See <a href="/guides/how-to-manage-homework/">how to manage homework</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Study plan:</strong> the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> or the <a href="/guides/study-plan-for-exams/">backward planning method</a> does the maths.</li>
    <li><strong>Study blocks:</strong> 25–50 minutes each, with a real break. The <a href="/guides/pomodoro-technique-for-students/">Pomodoro technique</a> is a good default.</li>
    <li><strong>Weekly review:</strong> pick a fixed time, like Sunday evening, and keep it to 15 minutes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="week">What a planned week looks like</h2>
  <p>Here is a sample week for a student with about 15 hours of classes and two exams in three weeks. Swap in your own subjects.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Classes</th><th scope="col">Planned study blocks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Monday</td><td>9:00–13:00</td><td>16:00 Chemistry topic 3 (new) · 17:00 homework due Wednesday</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>10:00–12:00</td><td>14:00 Maths practice set · 15:00 Chemistry flashcards (15 min self-test)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>9:00–14:00</td><td>17:00 History essay outline (due next Monday)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>11:00–13:00</td><td>15:00 Chemistry topic 4 · 16:00 Maths past-paper section</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Friday</td><td>9:00–11:00</td><td>12:00 History essay draft · evening off</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Saturday</td><td>—</td><td>10:00 Mock test (Maths) · 11:30 review mistakes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sunday</td><td>—</td><td>18:00 Weekly review (15 min) · rest</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Notice the pattern: new topics early in the week, a self-test in most sessions, the same subject coming back every few days (spacing), and a full rest evening. More examples are in our <a href="/guides/weekly-schedule-for-students/">sample weekly schedules for students</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="classtrack">How ClassTrack works as a study planner</h2>
  <p><a href={SITE.playUrl} rel="noopener">ClassTrack</a> is a free <strong>study planner app for students</strong> on Android, built to do the six steps above with as little typing as possible. It is used for school, college and university, and is not tied to any one country’s school system.</p>
  <figure style="margin:24px auto;max-width:260px;text-align:center">
    <img src="/img/screen-schedule.webp" alt="ClassTrack study planner app showing a colour-coded class timetable for the day" width="480" height="1040" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:24px" />
    <figcaption class="small muted">The ClassTrack schedule, built from a photo of a timetable.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">How ClassTrack does it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. Timetable</td><td><strong>AI timetable import</strong>: snap a photo, upload a PDF or paste text, check the classes it found, then save. Day, week and month views, with rooms, teachers, class links and one-off extra or cancelled classes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Exams</td><td>A countdown for every exam on the home screen, so the nearest one is always visible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. Homework</td><td>Assignments, to-dos and events grouped into <strong>Overdue, Today and Next 7 days</strong>, with reminders before they are due.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. Study plan</td><td><strong>AI learning plans</strong>: tell it what to learn and by when; it splits the topic into steps with time estimates and spreads them up to your date.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5. Study blocks</td><td>An <strong>AI study assistant</strong> that knows your real classes and exams, so it can suggest a revision schedule that fits around them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6. Review</td><td>Log marks and see your GPA on a <strong>4.0 or 10-point scale</strong>, plus a habit tracker and attendance by subject.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>What is free:</strong> the timetable, homework, exam countdowns, grades, habits and reminders, plus {SITE.free.aiMessages} AI messages a month, {SITE.free.timetableScans} AI timetable scans and {SITE.free.learningPlans} AI learning plans. <strong>ClassTrack Pro</strong> makes the AI unlimited and adds notes and flashcards, daily Google Calendar import, PDF/CSV export and a daily AI agenda. Pro is billed through Google Play in your local currency (<a href="/pricing/">see pricing</a>).</p>
  <p><strong>Honest limits:</strong> ClassTrack is Android-only for now (an iPhone version is coming soon) and has no web app, and the app is in English. If you need iPhone or web access today, read our <a href="/guides/best-study-planner-apps/">comparison of study planner apps</a> for alternatives.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href={SITE.playUrl} rel="noopener">Get ClassTrack free on Google Play</a></p>

  <h2 id="mistakes">5 mistakes that make students quit their planner</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Planning every hour.</strong> A plan with no gaps breaks on the first late class. Leave about 20% of each day empty.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing vague tasks.</strong> “Study physics” is hard to start. “Physics: 10 questions on circuits” is not.</li>
    <li><strong>Only planning, never testing.</strong> Rereading feels productive but is rated low-utility. Put a short self-test at the end of each block (Roediger &amp; Karpicke, 2006).</li>
    <li><strong>Cramming the last week.</strong> The same hours spread over several weeks are remembered much better (Cepeda et al., 2008).</li>
    <li><strong>Skipping the weekly review.</strong> Without it, the plan drifts away from real life and you stop trusting it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The goal is not a perfect plan. It is a plan you open every day and fix every week.</p>
</Guide>
